t is $A^2$?","options":["$P\\operatorname{diag}(4,49)P^{-1}$","$P\\operatorname{diag}(2,7)P^{-1}$","$P\\operatorname{diag}(4,14)P^{-1}$","$P^2\\operatorname{diag}(2,7)P^{-2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"552731679d505c44dcfa4b2c","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,3)$, which diagonalization is immediate?","options":["$P=0$, $D=A$","$P=A$, $D=I$","$P=I$, $D=0$","$P=I$, $D=A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"72ba2d648f3c30ce1d247513","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are eigenvectors placed in the columns of $P$ rather than chosen arbitrarily?","options":["The columns of $P$ are the diagonal entries of $D$.","The equation $AP=PD$ records $Av_i=λ_iv_i$ column by column.","Arbitrary columns always make $P^{-1}AP$ diagonal.","The columns of $P$ must be standard basis vectors only."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a358eb6901da5128a80490ff","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does diagonalization make computing $A^{k}$ easy?","options":["The inverse of $P$ disappears permanently.","The eigenvalues are replaced by row operations.","The power of every diagonal matrix is zero.","The middle factors $P^{-1}P$ cancel repeatedly."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1bacfbd381b3e8f5e2176720","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $P=[v_1\\ v_2]$, $D=\\operatorname{diag}(2,5)$, and $A=PDP^{-1}$. What is $Av_2$?","options":["$0$","$v_1+v_2$","$5v_2$","$2v_2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a0fbac102109f25f5374c814","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the order of the columns of $P$ matter only together with the order of the diagonal entries of $D$?","options":["Changing the order of columns always destroys invertibility.","Each eigenvector column must be paired with its corresponding eigenvalue.","The diagonal entries of $D$ must be sorted from small to large.","The order never matters, even if the pairings are changed."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"859bac8ee28af538e062f548","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_012_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"An $n\\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has independent eigenvectors $\\boldsymbol{p}_1,\\ldots,\\boldsymbol{p}_n$ with $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{p}_i=\\lambda_i\\boldsymbol{p}_i$. With $\\boldsymbol{P}=[\\boldsymbol{p}_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\boldsymbol{p}_n]$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_n)$, which equation always holds?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$","$\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}$","$\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{P}=\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}$","$\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6104e0f9598718401c7dd017","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_020_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A claimed diagonalization $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$ of a $2\\times2$ matrix uses $\\boldsymbol{P}$ with columns $(1,2)^{\\top}$ and $(-2,-4)^{\\top}$, both eigenvectors of $\\boldsymbol{A}$. What is wrong?","options":["The columns are dependent, so $\\boldsymbol{P}$ is not invertible.","The columns are not orthogonal, which diagonalization requires.","Nothing, since both columns are eigenvectors of $\\boldsymbol{A}$.","Nothing, since both columns are nonzero vectors."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b2fd8595d5455c6e58ff9442","time_limit":35}]}}